{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact draft5 ab44c9e9546fb6395b2882724d4df51fa895591f/tree2e28bd8c341e6b4df42e9b1d2f181398265fae46, notes/2026-10-19-record-and-event-identifiers.md. Part one read cold first in full, then the whole note. One documentation path against actual Main dba3e762; primary native effective/exact/direct promise,1/1artifactquery exhausted and assigned prepare. The following is the complete D5 finding set.\n\nD5-F1 [P1] Break the genesis hash cycle before commissioning section8 (lines518–539, especially529–532). Omitting the outer at/genesis member does not remove references to the same genesis from the entry's effects and sends. Today's Directory genesis creates membership/rules/destination. packages/platform/src/directory.ts:470 puts resolved.at in each child's Seed.creator; its create-rules at776 and create-destination at793 put the Directory reference in the recorded request body, and sibling IDs are hashes of those seeds. packages/derive/src/genesis.ts:207–212 places those sends in the genesis draft; scope/src/turn.ts:382–392 hashes the completed entry. Substituting SequenceRef{scope,genesis:G} gives G=Hash(genesis containing child.creator.genesis=G and body.directory.genesis=G), also through sibling IDs. The proposed ordinary content-hash construction cannot compute that reference. Specify how self references and child seeds are encoded before sealing, or defer their creation until after genesis, and cover genesis-created children in the adoption witness. Do not leave this as mechanical replacement of inc by genesis. This conclusion follows from exact source inspection plus the proposed substitution; no nonexistent migration was executed.\n\nD5-F2 [P1] Rehash Git objects instead of accepting the worked example's type lookup (262–269). git cat-file -t reports the object type; it does not establish that the bytes hash to the requested object ID. A private owned three-commit probe changed the middle loose object's bytes while keeping its original pathname. cat-file -t and cat-file commit both exit0; both rev-list root and first-parent-count checks also exit0 with the original root and correct count2. The changed predecessor actually hashes to f88ff657ef510da0fe1442abd0515def50073b3f, not its requested1a69369e9211d53714c60fd41fd536e8a0691cb1; fsck exits1/hash-path mismatch. Thus the example says levels0–2 pass on a chain that fails its own hash rule. Show actual raw commit rehash for the target and every traversed predecessor (or a precisely scoped integrity check), with first-parent root/count checks and graft/replace disabling. [Git cat-file](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-cat-file), [hash-object](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-hash-object) and [fsck](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-fsck) distinguish these operations.\n\nD5-F3 [P1] Bind a trusted anchor and qualify the chain claim (221–230). The walk can stop at an event the verifier already holds and trusts, yet the paragraph unconditionally says membership in the cited genesis is shown. Holding bytes, or trusting an event's signature, does not prove the prefix to that genesis. A forged short suffix can link to a cached unproved event and satisfy all traversed hashes without a path to the claimed genesis. Require an anchor bound to the same sequence, exact position and digest whose membership was previously proved; alternatively define explicit caller-asserted membership trust and state that the result is conditional on that trust. The report's generic trusts member helps disclose an assumption but does not itself establish the missing membership. Apply the same coverage distinction to authorship before an anchor.\n\nD5-F4 [P2] Supply the declared formats and fixed vectors that an outside verifier needs (section3;7;A.1.8–9;A.2.8–9). Exact candidate has no vectors/ files, although section3 says a token without all nine answers is not a format and appendixA calls dap1/git/git256 declared. dap1 vectors are expressly future. Its signature description lacks exact signed-input paths/envelope, key/signature encoding and the rule for unsigned genesis/delivery/outcome inputs; the Git paragraph names gpgsig/trailers but gives no precise payload extraction or pinned trailer profile. These are necessary byte operations, separate from deciding whose key is authorized. State exact profiles and publish independent immutable input/expected-output vectors, including signature/unsigned cases, or mark unsupported levels/formats explicitly and narrow the conformance claim until supplied. cases.json also needs an explicit per-case predecessor store/chain/anchor input: one event plus the common sequence cannot specify the required replaced-middle-chain case. [Git signature format](https://git-scm.com/docs/gitformat-signature) distinguishes a signed payload from the signature embedded in its object.\n\nD5-F5 [P2] Make reports and resolution interoperable on failures as well as success (247–256;302–334). An event failing level0 has no highest passed level and no chain endpoint, but reached/anchor have no specified null/absent/type rule. Missing predecessors, unsupported format/profile and a deliberately level-limited verifier also have no defined outcome distinction. Two implementations cannot produce the exact expected reports required by section7 without guesses. Give a precise result/report shape and examples for these cases. The HTTP binding also leaves base64 alphabet/padding, byte decoding, valid count/empty-page continuation and id/position/digest consistency unspecified. The sequence document lists type/serviceEndpoint for service entries but omits their required unique URI id; [DID Core1.0 section5.4](https://www.w3.org/TR/2022/REC-did-core-20220719/#services) requires all three. Give one complete valid representation and state how a citation DID URL dereferences to the bytes inside the transport envelope, with the chosen content type. See [DID URL dereferencing](https://www.w3.org/TR/2022/REC-did-core-20220719/#did-url-dereferencing). These details can be compact tables/examples; no discovery service or extra authority is requested.\n\nArchitecture: the sequence/event/address distinction, genesis naming, full citations, first-parent rule and host-independent byte checks are clear. Section8's new SequenceRef direction is assessed as the proposed design, not rejected for departing from draft4. Its genesis self-reference requires a real construction. Security: fields are correctly separated from membership, read authorization from verification and replay from byte authorship; forks/head/time/availability limits are explicit. F1–F3 currently prevent the stronger construction and verification claims. Simplification: the reduced terminology and informational appendix help, but the few exact byte/schema rules and vectors above cannot be replaced by prose references to implementations.\n\nValidation: exact28156byte note SHA256bf2feef1028d9009f70a5b6769dc531a3952ab4107601d9dc1692b47d06d5d1b; eight local links resolve; only this Markdown path differs from Main; no vector leaves. Synthetic Git probe uses AppleGit2.54.0 only in own new bare scratch directory, no real repository object/ref/source change. No gate repeated for this document review. The public Gitseq identifier reference, W3C DID Core/registry, RFC8785 and official Git documentation were checked; no claim that the remote example's557step graph or all Forge-specific authority rules were independently executed.\n\nClosed read/stable-copy grant: /private/tmp/checker-identifiers-d5-1o2ss7yj/checker-evidence-manifest.json,1124bytes,SHA256190bb9376b1a524fcbdc2b1de32b4c39c1beff20c8789d95b4a31b2c6b049777, and only its four exactly named owned regular leaves (note, synthetic-probe JSON, genesis reference analysis, validation). Originals permanently frozen; never execute/edit. No adjacent synthetic Git directory/object/source/tool/cache grant. No approval, implementation, landing, push or deployment by checker.","body":{"artifact":"git:s